Chapter: KeysSidecar
  cites UI chapter KeyInput

 Proof that a test can type.

 Every test that waits on a keystroke is skipped, and the skip reasons say
 so plainly -- "blocks waiting for keyboard input", "requires display +
 keyboard". The reason was never that the VM cannot deliver a key: codex-vm
 has taken a scancode timeline through `-keys-file` for as long as
 build/test-gui.ps1 has driven GUI apps with one. It was that the default
 battery had no sidecar for it, so the only input a test could ask for was
 `.stdin`, and `.stdin` cannot reach a keyboard read at all: it is pumped
 into the serial ring, and `poll-key` reads the PS/2 key cell. Two paths,
 and the battery only had the wrong one.

 This test drives the second path. Its `.keys` sidecar types A, B and
 Enter as Set-1 make codes (30, 48, 28); the loop below is the shape
 `idm-rlb-loop` uses, and it must report 65, 66 and stop.

 That it stops is the assertion, not a detail. A loop that reads
 `uefi-read-key` and compares against ASCII 13 for Enter never terminates,
 because Enter's make code is 28 -- which is exactly what the first-boot
 ceremony did until BACKLOG 7.14. `poll-key` is the translation, and this
 file is what fails if it is bypassed again.

 There is no fuel cap. A loop that never sees a key hangs until the
 harness's wall budget kills it, which is the honest failure: this test
 exists to prove keys arrive, so not arriving must be a failure and not a
 quietly truncated answer.

Section: Definitions

 Collect keycodes until Enter, exactly as the passphrase reader does.

  ks-collect : List Integer -> List Integer
  ks-collect (acc) =
    let ch = poll-key
    in if ch == 0 then ks-collect acc
    else if ch == 13 then acc
    else ks-collect (list-push acc ch)

  ks-show : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  ks-show (xs) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni (integer-to-text (list-at xs i))
      ks-show xs (i + 1) len
    end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let ks = ks-collect []
    in act
      ks-show ks 0 (list-length ks)
      print-line-uni "terminated"
    end
  end
